## Talking to the researcher

Everything written to disk here — conventions, handoffs, plans, evidence, field
notes — is written **for the next Claude session**: dense, terse, safe to assume
the reader will look up whatever it doesn't know. Keep it that way.

**Conversation with the researcher is the opposite, and assumes nothing.** In
chat: name the problem in plain words before naming the fix; expand an internal
term the first time it appears in a session (`invariant 9`, `#71`, a phase
number, a file path all mean nothing on their own); give the rationale, not just
the conclusion; and keep **"what I decided myself"** visibly separate from
**"what needs your call."** Comprehensive, but readable start to finish without
opening a file. Detail belongs in the files, which is what the files are for.

This rule lives in CLAUDE.md rather than in a convention because it applies to
every message and so has no trigger to fire on — conventions are read on demand,
and there is no moment at which "how to explain" becomes relevant, because it
always is.

